First, the failure mode (“instruction straddling two TOR regions”) is purely in the instruction‐fetch path – the code that actually splits out a fetch into two accesses when an instruction crosses a PMP region boundary, and then runs the PMP address check on both halves. In Ibex this lives entirely in the RTL, not in the cosim infrastructure. In particular, you need to look at

 1. The top‐level fetch stage (`ibex_if_stage.sv`) where the fetch is split,
 2. The PMP unit itself (`ibex_pmp.sv`) that issues the check,
 3. The PMP address‐check helper (`ibex_pmp_addrcheck.sv`) that computes TOR boundaries, and
 4. The core‐control wrapper (`ibex_ctrl.sv`) that ties PMP into the pipeline.

Those four files will need to be touched to make sure the IF logic requests *both* halves of a misaligned instruction and that each half gets properly checked against its PMP region.  

```  
hw/ip/ibex/data/rtl/ibex_if_stage.sv  
hw/ip/ibex/data/rtl/ibex_pmp.sv  
hw/ip/ibex/data/rtl/ibex_pmp_addrcheck.sv  
hw/ip/ibex/data/rtl/ibex_ctrl.sv  
```
