│                       1. What the problem is                       │
│                                                                    │
│ The issue is that in lockstep mode, the core_busy_o signal of the  │
│ non-lockstep core is used to gate the shared core clock. Since the │
│ lockstep core is clocked by the same gated clock, it can be turned │
│ off in the same cycle that the main core’s core_busy_o glitches    │
│ low.                                                               │
│                                                                    │
│ As a result, a transient low glitch on core_busy_o may stop the    │
│ lockstep core from sampling the faulty value, so the mismatch is   │
│ not detected and no major alert is raised.                         │
│                                                                    │
│                    2. Where the problem occurs                     │
│                                                                    │
│ The relevant behavior is in rtl/ibex_top.sv:                       │
│                                                                    │
│  • clock_en is derived from core_busy_q                            │
│  • clock_en drives the clock gate                                  │
│  • both u_ibex_core and u_ibex_lockstep use the gated clock clk    │
│                                                                    │
│ This is the problematic path:                                      │
│                                                                    │
│  • core_busy_q controls clock_en                                   │
│  • clock_en gates clk                                              │
│  • clk feeds both cores                                            │
│  • lockstep core may be disabled too early to compare the bad      │
│    core_busy_o                                                     │
│                                                                    │
│ The comparison logic in rtl/ibex_lockstep.sv is not the root       │
│ cause; it only compares sampled outputs, so if the lockstep core   │
│ is not clocked at the right time, the mismatch cannot be observed. │
│                                                                    │
│               3. Best practices to take into account               │
│                                                                    │
│  • Do not let the lockstep monitor be clock-gated by the signal it │
│    is supposed to verify.                                          │
│  • The checker/lockstep path should remain observable when the     │
│    monitored output changes.                                       │
│  • Keep the fix minimal and localized to the clock-gating/control  │
│    path.                                                           │
│  • Preserve existing behavior outside lockstep operation as much   │
│    as possible.                                                    │
│                                                                    │
│                     4. How to fix the problem                      │
│                                                                    │
│ The clock-gating enable used for the shared clock must be          │
│ decoupled from the potentially faulty core_busy_o signal when      │
│ lockstep checking is active.                                       │
│                                                                    │
│ A robust minimal fix is to ensure the lockstep comparison can      │
│ still be evaluated before the clock is gated off. In practice,     │
│ this means adjusting the clock-enable logic so the lockstep core   │
│ is not disabled in the same cycle as the main core’s core_busy_o   │
│ transition. The most appropriate location is rtl/ibex_top.sv,      │
│ where the shared clock is generated and distributed to both cores. │
