Based on the compilation error, it’s clear that our top‐level (“wrapper”) port definition for the RVFI’s pmpaddr field is 32 bits wide, but we’re driving it with the internal `pmp_addr_rdata` vector, which is only `PMP_ADDR_WIDTH` bits wide (e.g. 30 bits when G=3).  We have two basic ways to fix this:

1) change the wrapper so that it zero-extends the internal `PMP_ADDR_WIDTH`-bit vector out to 32 bits before connecting it to the RVFI port;  
2) change the CSR registers file so that it drives out a full XLEN-bit (`[31:0]`) pmpaddr (i.e. pad the low G bits with zeroes on every read), rather than just the truncated field.

Additionally, because the RVFI interface itself is declared as 32 bits wide for all XLEN‐bit CSRs, touching the RVFI package to allow parameterizing the CSR width to `PMP_ADDR_WIDTH` is another possible angle.  Finally, `PMP_ADDR_WIDTH` lives in our PMP parameter package, so if we do decide to push everything to use a single width everywhere, that include also needs a look.

From the provided skeletons, the four files that most directly participate in this mismatch (and so will need edits) are:

```  
core-v-cores/cv32e40s/bhv/cv32e40s_wrapper.sv
core-v-cores/cv32e40s/rtl/core/cs_registers.sv
core-v-cores/cv32e40s/rtl/core/rvfi_pkg.sv
core-v-cores/cv32e40s/rtl/include/pmp_pkg.svh
```
