│                       Phase 1: FIX ANALYSIS                        │
│                                                                    │
│                       1) What the problem is                       │
│                                                                    │
│ The FMA unit currently assumes that the third operand (operand_c,  │
│ the addend) is always in the destination/result format             │
│ (dst_fmt_i). This works for fused multiply-add/sub operations      │
│ where the accumulator/result share the same format, but it         │
│ prevents supporting some widening floating-point add/sub           │
│ operations from the RISC-V vector spec, specifically cases where   │
│ the addend is in the source format (src_fmt_i) while the result is │
│ wider.                                                             │
│                                                                    │
│ So the issue is not with the arithmetic itself, but with how the   │
│ FMA input operands are unpacked and interpreted.                   │
│                                                                    │
│                    2) Where the problem occurs                     │
│                                                                    │
│ The issue is centered in src/fpnew_fma_multi.sv, in the operand    │
│ unpacking logic inside always_comb begin : op_select:              │
│                                                                    │
│  • operand_c is currently unpacked using dst_fmt_q                 │
│  • info_c is also taken from info_q[dst_fmt_q][2]                  │
│                                                                    │
│ That means the addend is always treated as having the destination  │
│ format.                                                            │
│                                                                    │
│ Relevant lines:                                                    │
│                                                                    │
│  • operand_c = {fmt_sign[dst_fmt_q][2], ...}                       │
│  • info_c    = info_q[dst_fmt_q][2];                               │
│                                                                    │
│               3) Best practices to take into account               │
│                                                                    │
│  • Keep the change minimal and localized.                          │
│  • Preserve existing behavior for all current operations unless    │
│    explicitly extended.                                            │
│  • Avoid changing top-level interfaces unless necessary.           │
│  • Prefer a solution that is easy to propagate through the current │
│    decode/control structure.                                       │
│  • Make the new behavior explicit in the operation encoding so     │
│    existing operations remain unambiguous.                         │
│                                                                    │
│                     4) How to fix the problem                      │
│                                                                    │
│ The cleanest minimal fix is to introduce a new operation encoding  │
│ for the “wide addend” variant, e.g. ADDW, and make the FMA select  │
│ the addend format based on the operation:                          │
│                                                                    │
│  • ADD continues to use dst_fmt_i for operand_c                    │
│  • ADDW uses src_fmt_i for operand_c                               │
│                                                                    │
│ This avoids changing the top-level ports and keeps backward        │
│ compatibility.                                                     │
│                                                                    │
