│                       Phase 1: FIX ANALYSIS                        │
│                                                                    │
│                       1) What the problem is                       │
│                                                                    │
│ The current FMA implementation assumes that operand C (the addend) │
│ has the same floating-point format as the result (dst_fmt_i). This │
│ is correct for standard fused multiply-add operations, but it      │
│ prevents supporting some widening vector floating-point add/sub    │
│ operations where the addend is narrow and the result is wide.      │
│                                                                    │
│ In particular:                                                     │
│                                                                    │
│  • current behavior supports narrow + wide -> wide                 │
│  • missing behavior is narrow + narrow -> wide                     │
│                                                                    │
│ The issue is that operand C is always unpacked using dst_fmt_i, so │
│ the addend cannot be interpreted in src_fmt_i when needed.         │
│                                                                    │
│                    2) Where the problem occurs                     │
│                                                                    │
│ The key problematic location is in src/fpnew_fma_multi.sv, in the  │
│ op_select block:                                                   │
│                                                                    │
│  • operand_c is unpacked using dst_fmt_q                           │
│  • info_c is unpacked using dst_fmt_q                              │
│                                                                    │
│ This is the exact place where the addend format is fixed to the    │
│ result format.                                                     │
│                                                                    │
│ A second relevant place is the operation enumeration in            │
│ src/fpnew_pkg.sv:                                                  │
│                                                                    │
│  • there is no distinct operation encoding for an “add with        │
│    source-format addend”                                           │
│  • adding such an operation would allow routing the format choice  │
│    without changing top-level ports                                │
│                                                                    │
│               3) Best practices to take into account               │
│                                                                    │
│  • Keep the fix minimal and localized.                             │
│  • Preserve existing behavior for all current operations.          │
│  • Avoid changing top-level interfaces unless necessary.           │
│  • Prefer reusing existing datapaths and only extend control       │
│    encoding when needed.                                           │
│  • Ensure the change does not affect non-FMA or existing FMA       │
│    semantics.                                                      │
│                                                                    │
│                     4) How to fix the problem                      │
│                                                                    │
│ The cleanest minimal fix is:                                       │
│                                                                    │
│  1 Add a new operation enum entry, e.g. ADDW, to distinguish “add  │
│    with addend in src_fmt_i”.                                      │
│  2 Update fpnew_fma_multi.sv so that for this new operation:       │
│     • operand_c is unpacked using src_fmt_q                        │
│     • info_c is unpacked using src_fmt_q                           │
│  3 Keep the default ADD behavior unchanged, so existing users are  │
│    unaffected.                                                     │
│                                                                    │
│ This avoids changing module ports and provides the needed          │
│ flexibility with a small API extension.                            │
│                                                                    │
