│                       Phase 1: FIX ANALYSIS                        │
│                                                                    │
│                       1) What the problem is                       │
│                                                                    │
│ The test tb_axi_lite_regs sometimes fails only for certain random  │
│ seeds because the background rand_load/toggle_load process updates │
│ register bytes while the AXI read-response checker is              │
│ simultaneously sampling reg_q to build the expected read data.     │
│                                                                    │
│ That creates a race: the expected value is computed from a         │
│ register shadow that can change in the same time window as the     │
│ read handshake. As a result, the checker may expect                │
│ DEADBEEFDEADBEEF but observe a partially updated value such as     │
│ DE01BEEF, depending on the seed and scheduling.                    │
│                                                                    │
│                    2) Where the problem occurs                     │
│                                                                    │
│ The issue is in test/tb_axi_lite_regs.sv, specifically in:         │
│                                                                    │
│  • proc_generate_direct_load / toggle_load                         │
│    This continuously changes reg_d and reg_load with randomized    │
│    timing.                                                         │
│  • proc_check_read_data                                            │
│    This samples reg_q when master.ar_valid && master.ar_ready and  │
│    pushes the sampled data into the expected-response queue.       │
│                                                                    │
│ The failure happens because the expected data is derived from      │
│ reg_q without ensuring a stable snapshot relative to the AXI read  │
│ transaction, while toggle_load may update the same bytes           │
│ concurrently.                                                      │
│                                                                    │
│               3) Best practices to take into account               │
│                                                                    │
│  • Avoid sampling shared DUT state directly at a time when         │
│    concurrent stimulus may still modify it.                        │
│  • For testbenches, expected-value computation should be based on  │
│    a stable snapshot or on explicitly synchronized events.         │
│  • Keep the fix minimal and avoid changing the intended randomized │
│    behavior of the test unless necessary.                          │
│  • Do not over-constrain the random load process if the goal is to │
│    keep stress testing; instead, make the checker robust.          │
│                                                                    │
│                          4) How to fix it                          │
│                                                                    │
│ The best fix is to make the read-data checker use a stable         │
│ captured snapshot of register contents when a read address         │
│ handshake occurs, rather than relying on live reg_q values that    │
│ can change due to the concurrent random load process.              │
│                                                                    │
│ A minimal, precise fix is to protect the expected-data capture by  │
│ sampling reg_q into a temporary snapshot in the same checker       │
│ context, so the expected data is computed from a consistent view.  │
│ This avoids the race without removing the randomized load          │
│ behavior.                                                          │
│                                                                    │
