│  • observed: 64'hDE01BEEF... (one byte has already been changed)   │
│                                                                    │
│ This is a classic race condition between:                          │
│                                                                    │
│  • the test’s deterministic write/read sequence, and               │
│  • the random direct-load stimulus generated in parallel.          │
│                                                                    │
│                    2. Where the problem occurs                     │
│                                                                    │
│ The issue is in test/tb_axi_lite_regs.sv, specifically in the      │
│ readback check in proc_generate_axi_traffic:                       │
│                                                                    │
│  • the test writes the known value to address 0                    │
│  • then reads it back and asserts the exact value                  │
│  • meanwhile, proc_generate_direct_load launches toggle_load()     │
│    tasks that can modify reg_d/reg_load at arbitrary times         │
│                                                                    │
│ The relevant problematic interaction is between:                   │
│                                                                    │
│  • proc_generate_axi_traffic                                       │
│  • proc_generate_direct_load                                       │
│  • toggle_load()                                                   │
│                                                                    │
│ Even though the random load is intended as separate stimuli, it    │
│ can interfere with the fixed-value readback test.                  │
│                                                                    │
│               3. Best practices to take into account               │
│                                                                    │
│  • Avoid concurrent background stimulus modifying DUT/register     │
│    state during a deterministic sanity check.                      │
│  • Ensure deterministic checks are isolated from random traffic.   │
│  • If a test depends on a stable register value, prevent           │
│    asynchronous stimuli from changing that register until the      │
│    check is complete.                                              │
│  • Keep the fix minimal so it doesn’t alter the intended random    │
│    testing behavior more than necessary.                           │
│                                                                    │
│                     4. How to fix the problem                      │
│                                                                    │
│ The simplest and most robust fix is to prevent the random          │
│ direct-load process from running until after the initial           │
│ known-register write/read check has completed.                     │
│                                                                    │
│ A minimal approach is:                                             │
│                                                                    │
│  • gate toggle_load() so it waits for a synchronization signal     │
│    before starting random updates                                  │
│  • assert that signal only after the deterministic write/read test │
│    finishes                                                        │
│                                                                    │
│ This keeps the random load behavior intact for later random        │
│ testing, while guaranteeing the first readback is stable.          │
