│                       Phase 1: FIX ANALYSIS                        │
│                                                                    │
│                       1) What the problem is                       │
│                                                                    │
│ The test tb_axi_lite_regs sometimes fails depending on the random  │
│ seed because a background thread that performs random direct loads │
│ (rand_load / toggle_load) can update register bytes at the same    │
│ time the test is performing its “known register” readback check.   │
│                                                                    │
│ As a result, the data returned by the read is sometimes partially  │
│ overwritten by the random direct-load mechanism, causing the       │
│ assertion to fail even though the AXI register logic itself is     │
│ functioning correctly.                                             │
│                                                                    │
│                    2) Where the problem occurs                     │
│                                                                    │
│ The issue is in test/tb_axi_lite_regs.sv, mainly in these areas:   │
│                                                                    │
│  • The direct-load stimulus thread:                                │
│     • proc_generate_direct_load                                    │
│     • toggle_load                                                  │
│  • The known register write/read check:                            │
│     • proc_generate_axi_traffic                                    │
│     • specifically the read assertion at the known register check  │
│                                                                    │
│ The race is caused by toggle_load() starting immediately after     │
│ reset and continuing to update reg_d / reg_load concurrently with  │
│ the readback validation.                                           │
│                                                                    │
│               3) Best practices to take into account               │
│                                                                    │
│  • Avoid nondeterministic testbench races between stimulus         │
│    generation and scoreboarding/checking.                          │
│  • Keep the “known register” functional check isolated from        │
│    unrelated random stimulus.                                      │
│  • Preserve test intent: verify a deterministic write/read path    │
│    first, then run random stimuli separately.                      │
│  • Make minimal changes so the rest of the test behavior remains   │
│    intact.                                                         │
│                                                                    │
│                     4) How to fix the problem                      │
│                                                                    │
│ Prevent the random direct-load activity from interfering with the  │
│ deterministic register test.                                       │
│                                                                    │
│ The best minimal fix is to delay the start of the direct-load      │
│ toggling until after the known register read/write check has       │
│ completed. That way, the fixed-value assertion is evaluated before │
│ any random background updates can affect the register contents.    │
