│                       Phase 1: FIX ANALYSIS                        │
│                                                                    │
│                       1) What the problem is                       │
│                                                                    │
│ The test tb_axi_lite_regs occasionally fails depending on the      │
│ random seed because a background process (toggle_load) can update  │
│ the register bytes while the test is performing the “known         │
│ register” write/read check.                                        │
│                                                                    │
│ That means the expected read data 64'hDEADBEEFDEADBEEF can be      │
│ partially overwritten by the random direct-load stimulus,          │
│ producing mixed values like de01beef, which causes the assertion   │
│ to fail.                                                           │
│                                                                    │
│                    2) Where the problem occurs                     │
│                                                                    │
│ The issue is in test/tb_axi_lite_regs.sv, specifically in the      │
│ interaction between:                                               │
│                                                                    │
│  • proc_generate_axi_traffic: performs the deterministic           │
│    write/read check                                                │
│  • proc_generate_direct_load / toggle_load: continuously injects   │
│    random register loads in parallel                               │
│                                                                    │
│ The vulnerable area is the direct-load stimulus:                   │
│                                                                    │
│  • it starts independently and can begin toggling register bytes   │
│    before or during the deterministic readback check               │
│  • there is no synchronization to keep random loading inactive     │
│    until the known-register test finishes                          │
│                                                                    │
│                   3) Best practices to consider                    │
│                                                                    │
│  • Deterministic tests should not be influenced by unrelated       │
│    background random stimulus.                                     │
│  • If a test includes both directed and random phases, the         │
│    directed phase should be isolated or explicitly synchronized.   │
│  • Background stimulus should start only after the directed        │
│    sanity-check has completed.                                     │
│  • Keep the fix minimal and localized to avoid changing test       │
│    intent.                                                         │
│                                                                    │
│                          4) How to fix it                          │
│                                                                    │
│ Delay the start of the toggle_load background stimulus until after │
│ the known-register write/read verification is complete.            │
│                                                                    │
│ A minimal and robust approach is:                                  │
│                                                                    │
│  • in proc_generate_direct_load, wait for end_of_sim to be         │
│    deasserted/cleared and then wait until the directed traffic     │
│    phase is done before launching random load loops                │
│  • since proc_generate_axi_traffic sets end_of_sim <= 1'b1 after   │
│    the deterministic test and before the random run phase ends,    │
│    use this flag to gate the direct load stimulus                  │
│                                                                    │
│ This ensures the random direct loads do not interfere with the     │
│ initial expected read response.                                    │
│                                                                    │
