I looked at the XSim errors and traced them back to the fact that the interface-instantiation wrapper never passes the “real” request/response types into the generic‐typed mux, so SV falls back to the default “logic” and then fails when you try to do `slv_reqs_i.aw` etc.  You have two immediate levers:

  1. Add the missing type parameters (`.axi_req_t(axi_req_t), .axi_resp_t(axi_resp_t)`) wherever you actually instantiate the `axi_lite_mux` (your “intf” wrapper and TB).
  2. Or change the default gen‐parameters in the mux itself so that if nobody passes them they default to the right “axi_lite_req_t”/“axi_lite_resp_t” from your package.

Therefore the files to update are:

  • The mux‐module itself – so you can give it sane defaults.  
  • The interface wrapper where the mux is instantiated.  
  • The testbench (or any other top‐level) that instantiates the interface.

From your skeleton list, those correspond to the following full paths (most → least critical):

```  
rtl/axi_lite_mux.sv  
rtl/axi_lite_mux_intf.sv  
tb/axi_lite_mux_tb.sv  
```
